[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Memory Leaks, OutOfMemoryError Types, and Heap Dump Analysis in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Understanding how the Java Virtual Machine (JVM) manages memory is critical for building high-performance, resilient applications. When memory is mismanaged, applications suffer from memory leaks and fatal OutOfMemoryErrors (OOM)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Types of OutOfMemoryError"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space - Occurs when the object allocation request cannot be satisfied because the heap is exhausted."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: GC overhead limit exceeded - Thrown when the Garbage Collector spends too much time (over 98%) reclaiming very little memory (under 2%)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace - Triggered when the native memory allocated for class metadata is exhausted."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Requested array size exceeds VM limit - Happens when an application attempts to create an array larger than the available heap size."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Simulating a Java Memory Leak"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A memory leak in Java occurs when objects are no longer needed by the application, but the Garbage Collector cannot remove them because they are still referenced. Below is a classic example using a static collection."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class MemoryLeakExample {
    private static final List<Double> LEAKY_LIST = new ArrayList<>();

    public void populateList() {
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) {
            LEAKY_LIST.add(Math.random());
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MemoryLeakExample example = new MemoryLeakExample();
        while (true) {
            example.populateList();
        }
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Capturing Heap Dumps Automatically"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To diagnose OOM errors effectively, you must configure the JVM to automatically generate a heap dump when an OutOfMemoryError occurs. Pass the following flags to your JVM startup parameters:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError -XX:HeapDumpPath=/path/to/dumps/heap_dump.hprof"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Analyzing Heap Dumps"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once you have successfully captured a heap dump (.hprof file), you can analyze it using tools like Eclipse Memory Analyzer (MAT), VisualVM, or YourKit. Follow these steps to find the leak:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Open the heap dump file in Eclipse MAT and run the Leak Suspects Report."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Examine the Dominator Tree to identify which objects consume the largest percentage of retained heap."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Inspect the Garbage Collection Roots (GC Roots) path to see why vital references are being held onto."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Prevention"},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always clear collections, caches, and listeners when they are no longer required."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use weak references (java.lang.ref.WeakReference) for cache implementations where applicable."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor your application memory usage in production environments using APM tools."}]
